      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/barker-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1895 the Missouri, Kansas and Texas Railroad laid tracks that ran right through Barker.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/barker-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town once hosted an inn, a brick factory, a twine mill, a general store, a telephone company, a depot, two churches, a one‑room school, and a saloon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/barker-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1985 the Texas Antiquities Committee declared the LH7 Ranch home a state archaeological landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
